INT. NIGHT BUS DEPOT - WAITING ROOM - NIGHT

Fluorescent tubes buzz. Plastic seats bolted in rows. A vending machine hums, its glass fogged. A schedule board shows every departure as DELAYED.

The door opens. A gust of cold. LORENZA ABDULLAHI, 50s, in a long wool coat and a knit cap, drags a small wheeled suitcase over the threshold. She stops. Looks around. No one else. She chooses a seat near the machine and sits, the suitcase upright beside her like a patient child.

She takes out a thermos. Pours. Her hands shake slightly. She drinks.

A public address speaker clicks.

STATION ANNOUNCER (V.O.)
(over speaker, tinny)
Attention passengers. The ten-fifteen to Redding has been delayed. We apologize for the inconvenience.

Lorenza checks her watch. It is ten-forty.

STATION ANNOUNCER (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(over speaker)
New departure time: unknown.

She screws the cap on the thermos. Sets it in her lap. She looks at the empty seats, one by one, as if counting.

She opens her handbag. Takes out a folded letter. Reads the first line. Reads it again. Refolds it and slides it back.

The vending machine rattles and drops something into its tray, unprompted. Lorenza looks at it for a long moment. She stands, crosses to it, bends, and lifts out a packet of peanuts. She turns it over. Opens it. Eats one.

STATION ANNOUNCER (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(over speaker)
Passengers are advised to remain in the waiting area.

Lorenza returns to her seat. Smooths her coat over her knees. Sits very straight, and waits.
